From 5e74c37b7c5aae2ec3e70694975a3e5e330bca51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 04:38:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09889/11362] Mechanical backstop: a mutation marker may not
 reach a commit

The mutation-PROOF session shipped earlier today (REQ-OPS-MUTATION-PROOF-1) is
OPT-IN. An agent has to remember to wrap. A wrapper nobody calls is this
project's own named bug class: --check-targets shipped with no caller and ran
only when a human remembered to type it, which CLAUDE.md records as
trust-without-verification one level up from the thing it was written to defeat.

So this adds a hook that does not care whether a session was used. It watches
for the ACTUAL harm -- a mutated line reaching a commit. That line is valid
Python: it parses, imports, and clears ruff, mypy and every other hook in this
repository, while the conductor commits on its own schedule with hooks skipped.
Nothing else in the config would have stopped the observed incident, where a
marked no-op statement sat in arc_executable_world_model.py on the LIVE ARC
scored path.

scripts/mutation_marker_lint.py, wired as pre-commit `mutation-marker-lint` on
staged *.py. Design points, each with its reason:

  * Reads the INDEX via `git show :<path>`, not the working tree -- the question
    is what the COMMIT will contain, so an in-flight unstaged proof is not
    blocked while a staged-then-cleaned-on-disk marker still refuses.
  * Word-START boundary only. PERMUTATED passes; a suffixed marker does not.
    Requiring a word END too would let the suffixed form through, and the fail
    direction here is toward catching.
  * The marker token is imported from the module that defines it, never copied.
  * Four-entry allow-list, each file unable to do its job without the literal
    word, asserted by a test.
  * Every could-not-determine path REFUSES: unqueryable git, unreadable file,
    undecodable bytes.

Verified end-to-end through real pre-commit against the exact incident line.

RESIDUAL, stated rather than implied: a hooks-skipping commit bypasses this
entirely, and that is how the observed incident would have landed. The hook and
the session wrapper are COMPLEMENTS. The spec now carries a table naming which
of the five properties are mechanical (2: no marker in a commit; no commit while
a session is open) and which need an agent to invoke the session (3: exclusivity,
byte-identical restore, inert-run refusal). The REQ can no longer be read as
though wrapping were guaranteed.

TWO SELF-INFLICTED PROBLEMS, caught by this work's own tests and recorded
because the shape keeps recurring. `_marker()` resolved the marker's definition
through REPO, which tests point at a throwaway tree, so every test raised
FileNotFoundError. And the GUARD_TARGETS reason string this session added quoted
the literal marker, so the new lint fired on qa_layer_authenticity_audit.py and
would have refused every commit -- the same brick the sibling guard committed
hours earlier. Caught by test_the_real_repo_is_clean_under_this_lint, which
exists precisely because the sibling's version was found by hand.

SOURCE EXCLUSIONS MADE MACHINE-CHECKABLE. Both audits excluded from the ledger
report zero flagged findings today, so an UNSTATED exclusion would look identical
to a working inclusion: the ledger is silent either way and nobody could tell
which. EXCLUDED_SOURCES now names all six milestone-close audits with a written
reason each, and discover_milestone_close_audits() DERIVES the list from
research_conductor.py rather than hardcoding it, so a newly-wired audit cannot
drift into silence. The recorded answers: verifier_authenticity_audit.py keeps
its flagged set as a function local and artifact_convention_audit.py uses a
positional slice with no name -- neither can be imported, and re-declaring a copy
is the drift the module refuses on principle. Promoting either to a module
constant makes it a one-line addition.

--check-targets fired on the new hook before it was classified, as designed. It
is now in GUARD_TARGETS with the reason written down.

Spec: REQ-OPS-MUTATION-PROOF-2 with 6 SCENARIOs, plus the enforcement table
under REQ-OPS-MUTATION-PROOF-1. Tests 95 -> 122. Mutations M19-M24 each RED on
removal and GREEN on byte-identical restore, run in a worktree with the loaded
module verified by import first. M23 was vacuous on its first run -- every audit
is classified today, so the sweep could not distinguish "correctly empty" from
"always empty" -- and now injects an unclassified audit.

